findings.evidence:
lib/orders-recent-sync.mjs:76-80
```js
  } catch (err) {
    // Cursor stays put (sinceMs + overlap == the stored value) so the whole window is retried.
    setState({ lastSyncedAt: sinceMs + SYNC_OVERLAP_MS, lastError: err.message });
    throw err;
  }
```
The comment asserts `sinceMs + SYNC_OVERLAP_MS == the stored value`. That is only true when `state.last_synced_at` was truthy, because `sinceMs` is derived at line 51-53:
```js
  const sinceMs = state?.last_synced_at
    ? state.last_synced_at - SYNC_OVERLAP_MS
    : startedAt - SYNC_COLD_START_MS;
```
On the cold-start branch (`state.last_synced_at` falsy/0/undefined) `sinceMs = startedAt - SYNC_COLD_START_MS`, so the catch writes `lastSyncedAt = startedAt - SYNC_COLD_START_MS + SYNC_OVERLAP_MS` = `startedAt - 5min`, i.e. it ADVANCES the cursor from 'never synced' to '5 minutes ago' on a failed first run. The test at test/orders-recent-sync.test.mjs:59-69 only exercises the warm branch (`harness({ last_synced_at: base - 5000 })`) and asserts `h.state.last_synced_at === base - 5000`, so the cold-start error path is untested and the invariant is violated exactly where it matters most (first run after deploy/restart, when the poller has the largest window to cover).

server.mjs:12454-12460
```js
  setInterval(async () => {
    if (!dashboardActive()) return;            // quiet when nobody is looking
    if (_syncing || (Date.now() - _lastSyncAt) < FRESH_TARGET_MS) return;
    _syncing = true;
    try { await syncRecentFromShopify(); await reconcileOrderDeletions(); _lastSyncAt = Date.now(); }
    catch {} finally { _syncing = false; }
  }, 60 * 1000);
```
Before this change `syncRecentFromShopify` swallowed its own errors (the removed `catch (err) { console.error(...); setSyncState(...) }` block), so `reconcileOrderDeletions()` always ran and `_lastSyncAt` always advanced. The new `syncRecentOrders` rethrows (lib/orders-recent-sync.mjs:79), and the caller's `catch {}` is empty, so on any poller error the `await reconcileOrderDeletions()` on the same line is skipped and `_lastSyncAt` is NOT updated. The module header explicitly states the rethrow is intentional ('rethrown so callers/"Sync now" see the failure'), but the only caller in this diff is the scheduler, whose catch discards it.

test/orders-recent-sync.test.mjs:14-22
```js
  const fn = async (_q, { q, first, after }) => {
    calls.push({ q, first, after });
    const since = Date.parse(q.replace('updated_at:>', ''));
    const rows = orders.filter(o => Date.parse(o.updatedAt) > since).sort((a, b) => Date.parse(a.updatedAt) - Date.parse(b.updatedAt));
    const start = after ? Number(after) : 0;
    const slice = rows.slice(start, start + first);
    const end = start + slice.length;
    return { data: { orders: { edges: slice.map(node => ({ node })), pageInfo: { hasNextPage: end < rows.length, endCursor: String(end) } } } };
  };
```
The fake treats `after` as an integer index into the freshly-filtered `rows` array and returns `endCursor: String(end)`. Real Shopify returns an opaque base64 cursor that is only meaningful against the SAME query/filter set. The production code (lib/orders-recent-sync.mjs:74) passes `conn.pageInfo.endCursor` straight back as `after`, and the resume test at test/orders-recent-sync.test.mjs:46-57 relies on the fake's index semantics to make the second run pick up at row 50. Because the fake re-filters by `updated_at:>since` on every call and the cursor is an index into that filtered list, the test cannot detect a real-world failure mode: if the resume window's `since` changes between runs (which it does — the cursor is set to `orders[49].updatedAt` at line 83, so the next run's `since` is `orders[49].updatedAt - 60s`), the filtered `rows` array is a DIFFERENT set than the one the cursor was minted against, and a real opaque cursor would be invalid or would skip/duplicate rows.
